## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to design a robust cross-namespace orchestration primitive, with DAG scheduling, idempotent reconciliation, and cleanup semantics. It also touches RBAC, metrics, and testing at scale.

## Key Concepts

- CustomResourceDefinition and controller patterns
- DAG scheduling and dependency resolution
- Per-namespace isolation and quotas
- Idempotent reconciler design
- Finalizers and cleanup lifecycle
- Observability: conditions, events, metrics across namespaces
- Backoff strategies with jitter

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler structure
async function reconcile(dataMigrationPlan) {
  // Parse DAG and identify ready tasks
  const readyTasks = parseDAG(dataMigrationPlan.spec.tasks);

  for (const task of readyTasks) {
    // Check idempotency
    if (await jobExists(task.name)) {
      continue;
    }

    // Create Job with backoff strategy
    await createJob(task);

    // Add finalizer for cleanup
    await addFinalizer(dataMigrationPlan, task.name);
  }

  // Update status and conditions
  await updateStatus(dataMigrationPlan);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable governance mechanism spanning policy storage, reconciliation, and enforcement across many namespaces, while preserving auditability and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Namespace-scoped policy governance
- Central policy store with versioning
- Auto-generation of RBAC Roles, ResourceQuota, and LimitRange
- Real-time enforcement via admission webhook
- Drift detection and safe rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: reconcile NamespaceGuardPolicy and apply RBAC/limits across a namespace
async function reconcile(ns) {
  const policy = fetchPolicy(ns);
  if (!policy) return;
  ensureRBAC(ns, policy.rbac);
  ensureQuota(ns, policy.quota);
  ensureLimits(ns, policy.limits);
  audit(ns, policy.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this at scale across thousands of namespaces?
- How would you handle policy propagation latency and eventual consistency?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses design of policy-driven mutating webhooks, dynamic CRD-backed configuration, and production-grade reliability (fail-closed, caching, hot reload, observability).

## Key Concepts

- MutatingAdmissionWebhook lifecycle, failure modes, and timeouts
- Policy CRD design with namespace overrides and defaults
- In-memory policy cache and Kubernetes watch-based invalidation
- Observability: metrics (latency, patch count), tracing, and audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
function applyPolicyToPod(pod, policy) {
  const ns = pod.metadata.namespace;
  const nsPolicy = policy[ns] || policy["default"];
  if (!nsPolicy) return pod;

  const containers = (pod.spec.containers || []).map((c) => {
    const req = c.resources?.requests || {};
    const lim = c.resources?.limits || {};
    const newReq = { ...req, cpu: nsPolicy.maxCPU, memory: nsPolicy.maxMemory };
    const newLim = { ...lim, cpu: nsPolicy.maxCPU, memory: nsPolicy.maxMemory };
    return { ...c, resources: { requests: newReq, limits: newLim } };
  });
  return { ...pod, spec: { ...pod.spec, containers } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you version policies and handle breaking changes?
- How would you test webhook fail-closed semantics under policy backend latency?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical multi-tenant quotas, webhook-based enforcement, and observability at scale. It tests integration of ResourceQuota with a custom NamespaceQuota CRD, and the ability to reason about backpressure and fairness under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Namespace-scoped quotas and burst pools
- Mutating admission webhook and reconciliation patterns
- Custom controllers for usage accounting and alerts
- Observability: metrics, events, and tracing

